==========================================
DAY 19 NOTES — SSH Key-Based Authentication
Date: 17-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- Public key = shareable (like a padlock), Private key = secret (the only matching key)
- ssh-keygen -t ed25519 generates a modern key pair (private + .pub file)
- Private key file MUST have 600 permissions — SSH refuses to use it otherwise
- ssh-copy-id automatically copies your public key to a server's authorized_keys file
- Once set up, ssh user@host logs in with NO password prompt at all
- NEVER share your private key — only the .pub file ever leaves your machine
- Wrong permissions on ~/.ssh (700) or authorized_keys (600) on the server can silently break key login

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] ssh-keygen -t ed25519
[ ] checking/fixing key permissions with chmod
[ ] ssh-copy-id
[ ] confirming passwordless login works
[ ] viewing public key content with cat

fill this in after running commands yourself :
$ ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "my_email"
> 

$ ls -la ~/.ssh
> 

$ ssh-copy-id raven@<test-server-ip>
> 

$ ssh raven@<test-server-ip> (should now skip password)
> 


